<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dumat al-Jandal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Adummatu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dumat al-Jandal is an ancient oasis town in the Jawf region of northern Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, at the head of the Wadi Sirhan. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> as Adummatu, it was the centre of the Qedarite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the shrine of their gods, whose images <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> carried off in the 7th century BCE. It was later a Nabataean and Roman outpost and fell to Muslim forces in the 7th century; the Mosque of Umar and Marid castle survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dumat-al-jandal.jpg" alt="Dumat al-Jandal" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nora Ali at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_old_city_of_Adummatu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means Dumah of the Stone, and the town is also called Al-Jawf, the depression, after the Wadi Sirhan. It lies north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafud">Nafud</a> desert, 37 kilometres from Sakaka, within an oasis enclosed by the Dumat al-Jandal Wall. It stood where branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade route met, one linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> with Lower Mesopotamia, another linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> through the Wadi Sirhan with southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Some scholars identify it with Dumah, one of the twelve sons of Ishmael in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A triangular megalithic platform 35 metres long, surrounded by burials and dating to the 6th millennium BCE, was reported in 2020. Assyrian inscriptions of 845 BCE call the town the capital of an Arab kingdom sometimes named Qedar, and the Esarhaddon Prism of 673–672 BCE calls it the strong city of the Arabians. Five ruling queens are known, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabibe">Zabibe</a>, Šamši, Tabūʿa and Teʾelḫunu, the last also high priestess of Atarsamain; the town had an important temple of Ishtar.</p><p>Roman-Nabataean pottery shows a prosperous community under the Nabataeans, and a large open-air Nabataean banqueting hall, used from the late 1st century BCE, was found in 2011. Porphyry reports that a boy was sacrificed there each year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> brought Dumah into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in 106, and it was for over four centuries the easternmost settlement of the Limes Arabicus. Its market fairs, disputed between Arab clients of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, specialised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and prostitution.</p><p>Lying about fifteen days' march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, the town was raided three times on Muhammad's orders. The expedition of July 626 found the Ghaṭafān fled, and in 630 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> captured it for the new Islamic state; in 631 he destroyed the idol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadd">Wadd</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dumat al-Jandal is the historical capital of what is now al-Jouf Province of Saudi Arabia. The Umar Mosque, wrongly attributed to Caliph Umar, has a stone prayer hall and a tapering minaret and faces Marid Castle across a street. The nearby Al-Dar'i Quarter of stone houses and lanes, dating to the middle Islamic age, survives from the old town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dumat al-Jandal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their centre was the oasis of <b>Adummatu</b>, modern Dumat al-Jandal, which held the shrine of their gods.</blockquote><a href="/state/qedarites">Qedarites</a></li></ul></section>
